How the Future License conversion works
---------------------------------------

Each release of the platform core is published under FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0. On
the second anniversary of the date a given release is made available, that
release converts to the Apache License, Version 2.0. The Apache 2.0 text
used by the conversion is reproduced verbatim in LICENSE-APACHE for
transparency.

In practice this means:

  - Active development always lives under FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0.
  - Each release has a fixed expiry: two years to Apache 2.0.
  - The most recent release is always at most two years away from being
    plain Apache 2.0 — even if upstream development stops, the codebase
    eventually becomes fully OSI-open.

Two-license landscape during the FSL window
-------------------------------------------

Pre-existing releases v0.0.1 through v0.1.96 (plus the v0.1.0-mvp milestone
tag) were published under FSL-1.1-MIT, the previous base license choice
recorded in ADR 0001. Per ADR 0032, those releases retain their original
terms — their 2-year conversion clock continues running independently and
they convert to the MIT License (not Apache 2.0) on their own anniversaries.
LICENSE-MIT in the repository root preserves the MIT text for that purpose.

Only versions published from the ADR 0032 migration commit forward convert
to Apache 2.0.

