Why FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0?
-----------------------

The FSL Permitted-Purpose clause restricts other parties from offering the
platform as a competing managed service while it is under active
development. After two years, that protection naturally relaxes as the
release converts to Apache 2.0. This approach follows the model adopted
by Sentry in 2023 (which also uses FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0).

The Apache 2.0 conversion target was chosen over MIT for the platform core
because the project orchestrates Cilium (eBPF/CNI), SPIFFE/SPIRE, OpenBao,
Kamaji, and confidential-computing primitives (Kata/CoCo on Intel TDX,
AMD SEV-SNP, ARM CCA) — a domain with real patent footprint where Apache's
explicit patent grant (§3) and patent-retaliation clause are more than
ceremony. Apache 2.0 also aligns with the Kubernetes / CNCF ecosystem
convention and provides an explicit trademark disclaimer (§6). See ADR 0032
for the full rationale.

